{# version: 1 #}
Position a research paper against the work it builds on. Use only what is given below: the
paper's abstract and contributions, the paper's own paragraphs that cite these references
(each starts with an id in brackets), and each reference's title and abstract (marked R1, R2..).

Return:
- "summary": 2-4 sentences on what is new in this paper relative to these references. Say
  only what the paper's text supports; do not praise it.
- "positions": for each reference the paper's text says something about, one entry:
  - "ref": its marker (R1, R2, ...), exactly as given;
  - "relation": "extends" (builds on it), "uses" (uses it as a component, dataset or tool),
    "compares_to" (reports results against it) or "differs_from" (says it does something else);
  - "statement": one sentence on how this paper relates to it;
  - "quote": ONE sentence copied character-for-character from the paper's paragraphs below
    that supports the statement, or "" if they do not say;
  - "chunk_id": the id in brackets of the paragraph the quote comes from.
Leave out references the paper's text says nothing about. Do not invent markers, and in
"summary" and "statement" name references by title, not by marker.

Paper: {{ title }}

Abstract:
{{ abstract or "(not available)" }}

Contributions (as extracted):
{% for c in contributions -%}
- {{ c }}
{% else -%}
(not available)
{% endfor %}

References:
{% for r in references -%}
[{{ r.marker }}] {{ r.title }}{% if r.year %} ({{ r.year }}){% endif %}
{{ r.abstract }}

{% endfor -%}

The paper's paragraphs that cite them:
{{ text }}
